Title: The Night Ferry
Credit: Written by
Author: Test Fixture (original, for the Studio's tests)

EXT. DOVER HARBOUR - DAY

Rain drives across the white cliffs. Lorries queue for the ferry. MARA OKAFOR (34), a ship's engineer in a battered waxed jacket, jogs along the quay in muddy trainers, a toolbag over her shoulder.

She checks her Apple Watch. Late.

A Border Force officer, PRIYA (40s), waves her through the barrier.

PRIYA
Your dad's been asking for you. Third time this week.

MARA
Tell him I'm working. Tell him I'm always working.

INT. MARA'S LAND ROVER - MOVING - DAY

Mara drives the old Land Rover Defender through Dover's narrow streets, wipers thrashing. Her phone, clipped to the dashboard, lights up: DAD.

She lets it ring out. Takes a swig from a can of energy drink.

INT. TOM'S COTTAGE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

A cramped kitchen above the sea. TOM HALE (68), retired lighthouse keeper, Mara's father, makes tea in a chipped Brown Betty pot. His old sheepdog, BISCUIT, watches from the rug.

Mara enters, dripping.

TOM
You'll catch your death.

MARA
You called four times.

TOM
A man came asking about the Calais crossing. About you.

He slides a business card across the table. A French police crest.

INT. CROSS-CHANNEL FERRY - ENGINE ROOM - NIGHT

Heat and noise. Mara works under a diesel engine the size of a bus. Steam vents. Her laptop, balanced on a crate, shows a temperature graph climbing into the red.

JULIEN ROUX (40s), a French detective in a rain-dark overcoat, ducks through the hatch.

JULIEN
(in French; subtitled)
Madame Okafor? Lieutenant Roux, police judiciaire.

MARA
(in French; subtitled)
You're in my engine room, Lieutenant.

JULIEN
(in English)
Then we will talk loudly.

EXT. CROSS-CHANNEL FERRY - CAR DECK - NIGHT

A storm hits mid-Channel. The ferry pitches. Forty PASSENGERS shelter by their cars as chains strain. A child, AMY (8), clutches a stuffed rabbit.

A lorry's chains snap. The lorry slides. Mara dives, drags Amy clear as the trailer slams into the rail.

VFX: the ferry seen from above, tiny in the black storm.

EXT. CALAIS PORT - DAWN

France. Grey light, gulls, the town hall clock tower beyond the docks. Julien leads Mara past customs sheds to an unmarked Peugeot.

JULIEN
Your ferry carries something every Thursday. Not lorries. Not people.

MARA
Engines don't lie. Somebody's added weight below the waterline.

INT. CALAIS POLICE STATION - INTERVIEW ROOM - DAY

Strip lights. Bad coffee in paper cups. Julien spreads photographs: the same container, three different weeks.

JULIEN
Your father kept the lighthouse log thirty years. Every ship, every light.

MARA
He's not part of this.

JULIEN
Everybody in Dover is part of this.

EXT. DOVER - WHITE CLIFFS PATH - DAY

Mara and Tom walk the cliff path with Biscuit. The Channel spreads out below, ferries crossing like toys.

TOM
I wrote down a ship that wasn't on any manifest. 1998. I told the coastguard. Nobody came.

MARA
And you kept writing it down.

TOM
That's the job. You watch. You write it down.

INT. SMUGGLERS' WAREHOUSE - TANGIER, MOROCCO - NIGHT

Hot, dusty, lit by a single bulb. MALIK (50s) and two HENCHMEN load sealed crates into a shipping container. Malik speaks Arabic into a satellite phone.

MALIK
(in Arabic; subtitled)
Thursday. The English ferry. As always.

A handgun rests on the crate lid beside a bottle of local beer.

EXT. DOVER HARBOUR - CONTAINER STACKS - NIGHT

Rain. Floodlights. Mara slips between container stacks with a torch. Julien follows, pistol drawn.

A FORKLIFT roars out of the dark. Mara rolls under a trailer; the forks spear the container behind her. Sparks.

The driver flees on a motorbike. Julien fires twice; the bike skids, the RIDER (STUNT) slides across the wet concrete into the harbour.

JULIEN
Call it in!

MARA
(into phone)
Coastguard, this is Mara Okafor at Eastern Docks, man in the water.

INT. TOM'S COTTAGE - KITCHEN - LATER

Tom opens a biscuit tin full of old logbooks. Mara and Julien read by lamplight, mugs of tea going cold. Biscuit snores.

JULIEN
(reading)
The same hull number. 1998, 2009, 2024.

MARA
Twenty-six years of Thursdays.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE - SOUTH FORELAND - DUSK

The disused lighthouse above the cliffs. Tom climbs the spiral stair one last time, Mara behind him. At the top, he relights the old lamp with a modern LED floodlight Mara has wired in.

The beam sweeps the Channel and catches the smugglers' ship, running dark.

TOM
There you are.

EXT. ENGLISH CHANNEL - NIGHT

Border Force cutters close on the dark ship, blue lights flashing, spray over the bows. A helicopter's searchlight pins the deck.

INT. CROSS-CHANNEL FERRY - BRIDGE - DAWN

Mara stands on the bridge as the ferry noses into Dover. Julien beside her with two takeaway coffees.

JULIEN
In France we would give you a medal.

MARA
In England we'll give me a pay rise. Possibly.

She takes the coffee. The white cliffs fill the window.

FADE OUT.
